From: deepseek-v4-cmp170hx
Subject: [PATCH 0005a] sparse_attn_indexer: the prefill top-k torch fallback that
 patch 0006 rewrites but nothing ever added

MUST BE APPLIED BEFORE 0006. The name sorts between 0005 and 0006 so that a
plain `for p in patches/*.patch` loop gets the order right.

Patches 0001-0006 as originally published were INCOMPLETE, and in two ways:

  1. `_top_k_per_row_prefill_torch()` and `_prefill_topk_needs_torch_fallback()`
     were called at four sites and defined nowhere.
  2. 0006 does not ADD the fallback branch - it REWRITES one, turning
     `if _prefill_topk_needs_torch_fallback():` into an `elif` and carrying
     `_top_k_per_row_prefill_torch(` as unchanged context. The base
     (haosdent/vllm@dsv4-flash-a100, f8ea5bb) has a bare, unconditional
     `ops.top_k_per_row_prefill(...)` with no branch at all.

So supplying only the two definitions is not enough - 0006 still fails on the
`if` it expects to find. This patch adds both the definitions and the branch,
which is the state 0006 was authored against. Reported in issue #1 by @fouvy,
diagnosed by @snoby.

WHY THE FALLBACK EXISTS - DO NOT STUB IT OUT ON SM8x.

`ops.top_k_per_row_prefill`'s histogram path (taken by rows with more than
`topk_tokens` candidates) can leave part of its dynamic-shared-memory output
uninitialised and copy it out as indices. Downstream,
`compute_global_topk_indices_and_lens` treats any index >= 0 as valid and
dereferences it into the KV block table. Upstream added this fallback for SM12x
in vllm-project/vllm PR #49897; we enable it for SM8x too because on 4x CMP
170HX it reproduces as `Xid 31 MMU Fault ... ACCESS_TYPE_VIRT_WRITE` killing a
worker on prefills above roughly 128k tokens (123k passes).

`_prefill_topk_needs_torch_fallback()` returns True on sm_80 BY DESIGN. This is
unrelated to patch 0001, whose `persistent_topk` gate is precautionary and whose
originating report was retracted - that retraction does NOT apply here.
Override with VLLM_DSV4_PREFILL_TOPK_TORCH=0 (force the CUDA kernel) or =1
(force the fallback anywhere).

Ordering contract: the downstream sparse-attention kernels iterate selected KV
positions in ASCENDING POSITION order, whereas torch.topk returns score order.
`_top_k_per_row_prefill_torch` sorts accordingly and pads short rows with -1 at
the tail. A naive mask+torch.topk reimplementation runs but feeds wrongly
ordered indices downstream.

VERIFIED: from a pristine checkout of f8ea5bb, applying 0001 -> 0005a -> 0006
produces zero rejects and reproduces our live in-production file byte-for-byte
(md5 96380027dd74c5913b6c4aeca6b25b02).
